---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site, localBusinessSchema } from '../../config/site';

const procedureSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "MedicalProcedure",
  "name": "Dental Implants in Long Beach",
  "description": "Spring St. Dentistry provides dental implants in Long Beach — call to find out if you're a candidate.",
  "procedureType": "https://schema.org/Therapeutic",
  "provider": {
    "@type": "Dentist",
    "name": "Spring St. Dentistry",
    "address": {
      "@type": "PostalAddress",
      "addressLocality": "Long Beach"
    }
  }
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Dental Implants Long Beach | ${site.name}`}
  description="Spring St. Dentistry provides dental implants in Long Beach — call to find out if you're a candidate."
  schema={[localBusinessSchema, procedureSchema]}
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 md:py-24">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-primary mb-4">
      <a href="/services" class="hover:underline">Services</a>
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-neutral-dark mb-4 leading-tight">
      Dental Implants in Long Beach
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-neutral-mid mb-8 leading-relaxed">
      Spring St. Dentistry provides dental implants in Long Beach — call to find out if you're a candidate.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-neutral-mid leading-relaxed">
      <p class="mb-4">A dental implant is a permanent tooth replacement that restores both the root and the visible crown — unlike a bridge or denture, which sit on top of the gum. If you have a missing tooth that's affecting how you eat, speak, or feel about your appearance, an implant is often the most complete long-term solution available. Dr. Anthony Hoang at Spring St. Dentistry in Long Beach provides dental implant treatment for patients who are missing one or more teeth and want a result that looks and functions like a natural tooth.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">The implant itself is a small titanium post placed into the jawbone. Over a period of months, the post bonds with the surrounding bone through a process called osseointegration, creating a stable foundation. A dental crown is then attached on top, completing the restoration. The finished implant blends in with your surrounding teeth and doesn't require the adjacent teeth to be filed down, which a traditional bridge would require.</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-dark mt-8 mb-4">Who Is a Good Candidate for Dental Implants</h2>
      <p class="mb-4">Most adults with a missing tooth or teeth are potential candidates, but a few factors matter. Sufficient bone density in the jaw is important, because the implant needs solid bone to anchor into. Healthy gums are also necessary — active gum disease should be treated before implant placement. Certain medical conditions and medications can affect healing, so a full review of your health history is part of the evaluation process.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">Younger patients whose jawbones are still developing are generally not candidates yet. Smokers can still receive implants, but smoking slows healing and can affect long-term success, so it's something to discuss honestly at your consultation.</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-dark mt-8 mb-4">How the Dental Implant Process Works</h2>
      <p class="mb-4">The process typically takes place in stages over several months, with most of the timeline devoted to healing rather than active treatment.</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 my-4 space-y-2">
        <li>**Consultation and imaging:** Dr. Hoang examines the area, reviews X-rays or 3D scans, and determines whether the bone volume is sufficient.</li>
        <li>**Implant placement:** The titanium post is surgically placed into the jawbone. Local anesthesia is used, so the procedure itself is not felt. Mild soreness in the days afterward is normal and manageable with over-the-counter pain relief.</li>
        <li>**Osseointegration:** The implant is left to bond with the bone over roughly three to six months, depending on the individual.</li>
        <li>**Crown placement:** Once the implant is stable, an abutment and custom crown are attached. The crown is made to match the shape and color of your surrounding teeth.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mb-4">The total number of appointments varies, but patients can expect several visits spread across a few months.</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-dark mt-8 mb-4">What to Expect After the Procedure</h2>
      <p class="mb-4">After implant placement, most people return to normal activity within a day or two. The surgical site should be kept clean, and Dr. Hoang will provide specific aftercare instructions. Soft foods are recommended for the first week or so. Swelling and tenderness typically resolve within a few days.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">Once fully healed and restored, an implant is cared for exactly like a natural tooth — brushing, flossing, and regular dental check-ups. With consistent care, implants can last many years. They also help preserve the jawbone in the area of the missing tooth, which tends to shrink over time when a tooth root is absent. Keeping the implant stable also prevents neighboring teeth from drifting out of position.</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-dark mt-8 mb-4">Getting Started at Spring St. Dentistry in Long Beach</h2>
      <p class="mb-4">If you're considering an implant and want to understand whether it's the right option for your situation, the first step is a consultation. Patients in Long Beach can call Spring St. Dentistry at **(562) 420-8578** to schedule an evaluation with Dr. Hoang.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">---</p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-dark mt-8 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <p class="mb-4">**How long does a dental implant last?** With proper brushing, flossing, and regular dental visits, dental implants can last many years and often decades. The crown on top may eventually need replacement due to normal wear, but the implant post itself is designed to be permanent.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">**Does the implant procedure hurt?** The placement is done under local anesthesia, so you shouldn't feel pain during the procedure. Soreness and mild swelling in the days after are common and typically managed with over-the-counter pain relievers.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">**How long does the whole process take from start to finish?** Most patients complete the process in roughly four to eight months, with the majority of that time being the healing period after implant placement rather than active treatment appointments.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">**Can an implant replace more than one tooth?** Yes. Individual implants can replace individual missing teeth, but implants can also be used to anchor bridges or support full-arch restorations when multiple teeth are missing.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">**Is a dental implant covered by insurance?** Coverage varies significantly by plan. Some dental insurance policies cover a portion of implant treatment; others do not. It's worth calling your insurance provider directly to ask about your specific benefits before your consultation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-10 flex gap-4 flex-wrap">
      <a href="/schedule" class="btn-primary">Schedule a Consultation</a>
      <a href="/services" class="btn-secondary">View All Services</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
